<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zawi ibn Ziri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1019</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zawi ibn Ziri was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> leader of the Zirid family who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> to serve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. During the civil war after 1009 he seized the district of Elvira around 1013 and moved its centre to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Granada. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> around 1019, leaving the kingdom to his nephew Habbus.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zawi was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziri-ibn-manad">Ziri ibn Manad</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Fatimid caliph al-Mansur bi-Nasr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirid dynasty</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> in 944 and built the city of El Achir. Ziri was killed fighting the Zenata in 970, and his head was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and displayed in the market place. Zawi's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buluggin-ibn-ziri">Buluggin ibn Ziri</a> then governed the Maghreb for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. Under Buluggin's son al-Mansur the family was troubled by internal tensions, and after failed attacks against al-Mansur's son and successor Badis ibn Mansur in 999 and 1000, Zawi left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> with his son, nephews and followers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>, mayor of the palace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, welcomed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> as support for his plan to strip Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> of all authority, and they became the main support of him and his sons Abd al-Malik al-Muzaffar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-sanchuelo">Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo</a>. In the civil war between the Spanish Muslims and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> troops, Zawi led Sanhaja, Zenata and other Berbers against Córdoba to install Sulayman ibn al-Hakam as caliph. The Berbers plundered the city and attacked its leading families, and Zawi took down his father's head from the top of the citadel so that it could be buried with his body.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As discord spread, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> took over the district of Elvira. Zawi soon moved his capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-elvira">Madinat Ilbira</a>, near modern Atarfe, to the more defensible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, until then a small settlement on the right bank of the Darro river, and so founded the modern city as capital of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a>. In April 1018 Ibn Hazm and the followers of the Umayyad pretender al-Murtada tried to take Granada and were heavily defeated.</p><p>Distressed by his countrymen's excesses in the civil war and fearing divine vengeance, Zawi resolved to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, naming his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habbus-ibn-maksan">Habbus ibn Maksan</a> as his successor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muizz-ibn-badis">Al-Mu'izz ibn Badis</a>, ruler of the Zirids in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, welcomed him with honour, and Zawi buried his father's head in his tomb. The Zirids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> ruled until 1238.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zawi ibn Ziri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1013 the Sanhaja Berber leader <b>Zawi ibn Ziri</b>, a veteran of the caliphal wars, took over the district of Elvira and moved its centre to the more defensible hill of Granada.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-granada">Taifa of Granada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.78,3.06&amp;year=1009" aria-label="Algiers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Zawi ibn Ziri"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5209 4419h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5209 4419h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.78,3.06&amp;year=1009">What was here in 1009? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
